<Legal
  title="Security"
  description="How this site is built to minimise risk, the security commitments BreatheFree must meet before release, and how to report a vulnerability."
  eyebrow="Trust"
  intro="This page describes two different things: how this website is secured today, and the security bar our products must clear before anyone can use them."
  updated="2026-08-29"
  contactMailbox="security"
  scopeNote="We make no claim to hold any security certification. Where this page describes controls for BreatheFree, it describes release requirements that have not yet been met — not controls that are in place today."
>
  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    The site is deliberately boring, because boring is the most reliable
    security property available to a marketing site.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Static output.</strong> Every page is pre-rendered HTML. There is no
      application server, no database, and no server-side code that processes your
      input — so there is no query to inject into and no session to steal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No third-party code.</strong> The site loads no external scripts, fonts,
      images or stylesheets. A supply-chain compromise of an analytics or font vendor
      cannot reach visitors of this site, because there is no such vendor in the page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No accounts, no cookies.</strong> There is nothing to log in to and
      no session state, so there are no credentials here to lose.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Minimal data.</strong> The early-access form composes a message in your
      own email client rather than posting to a server we run. We hold what you chose
      to send, and nothing else.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What BreatheFree must satisfy before release</h2>
  <p>
    BreatheFree handles information people would not want disclosed. It is not
    released, and it will not be released until the following are in place and
    independently checked:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Data-flow mapping, threat modelling and a privacy impact assessment
      completed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Authentication built on OAuth/OIDC with short-lived tokens and secure
      device storage.
    </li>
    <li>Protected data encrypted in transit and at rest.</li>
    <li>
      Consent versioning, purpose limitation and revocation enforced on the
      server, not merely reflected in the interface.
    </li>
    <li>
      Verified export and deletion, with completion that can be audited rather
      than assumed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Sensitive notification text off by default, so a lock screen never
      discloses someone's health status.
    </li>
    <li>
      Crash reporting and diagnostics that carry no health-entry payloads.
    </li>
    <li>
      Abuse prevention, rate limiting, monitoring and a written
      incident-response procedure.
    </li>
    <li>
      Independent penetration testing of the mobile application, the API and the
      cloud environment.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These are release gates. If one is not met, the release does not happen.
  </p>

  <h2>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
  <p>
    If you believe you have found a security issue in this website or in
    anything else we run, please tell us at <SupportEmail mailbox="security" />.
    The same contact is published in our <a href="/.well-known/security.txt"
      >security.txt</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>Please include, as far as you can:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>What the issue is and where you found it.</li>
    <li>Steps that reproduce it.</li>
    <li>What an attacker could achieve with it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We will acknowledge your report, keep you informed while we investigate, and
    credit you if you would like to be credited. Please give us a reasonable
    opportunity to fix an issue before disclosing it publicly, and please do not
    access or modify anyone else's data, degrade our service, or run automated
    scanning that would.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not currently run a paid bug-bounty programme. We are grateful anyway.
  </p>
</Legal>
